<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="section-improper-integration" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Improper Integration
  </title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this section, we expand the kinds of functions we consider as the integrands of Riemann integrals. Thus far, we have only allowed Riemann integrals of the form
      
      <md>
        \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
      </md>,
      
      where <m> f \colon \clint{ a }{ b } \to \R </m> is a Riemann integrable (and therefore bounded) function defined on a closed interval. In this section, we relax both of these conditions while maintaining a grasp on some of the nice properties of the theory of Riemann integration.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <xi:include href="./subsection-exhaustion-by-compact-intervals.ptx"/>

  <xi:include href="./subsection-tests-for-integral-convergence.ptx"/>

  <conclusion>
